%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                       Dreams and Waking                       %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% Player character in the real world
#p-real
(animate *)
(current player *)
(* is #in #nabiyunus)
(descr *) You look like a very respectable archaeologist-in-training. Maybe a bit frazzled, and more than a bit sleep-deprived, but you’ll have time to sleep once your thesis is done. Until then, the research comes first.

%% Player character in the memory world
#p-memory
(animate *)
(supposed to be nowhere *)
(descr *) %% Individual memory protagonists get their own descriptions
	(current room $Room)
	(protagonist description for $Room)
(descr *) %% Not in a specific memory
	You look exactly like yourself—(no space)(emph {too}) much like yourself, really. It’s like you’ve been clumsily photoshopped into your surroundings. Your outlines are just a bit too sharp, your colors a bit too saturated.

%% For readability
(dreaming)	(current player *)

%% The actions to go back and forth
(grammar [divine [single]] for [divine $])
(grammar [divine] for itself)
(perform [divine])
	Divine what?
	(asking for object in [divine []])
~(action [divine $Obj] requires $Obj to be touched)

(prevent [divine $])
	(dreaming)
	Divining is how you enter a memory; to get back out again, you just have to (link) {wake up} .
(prevent [divine $Obj])
	(current player $Obj)
	You are, fortunately, still in the realm of “human” rather than “human remains”.
(prevent [divine $Obj])
	(animate $Obj)
	While these humans are surely long-dead, a memory unfortunately doesn’t count as their “remains” for the purposes of divining.
(prevent [divine $Obj])
	~($Obj divines to $)
	(The $Obj) (doesn’t $Obj) seem to be the remains of a human in situ; that’s what you need for proper forensic necromancy.

(perform [divine $Obj])
	($Obj divines to $Room)
	(narrate divining $Obj)
	(now) (#p-memory is #in $Room)
	(select player #p-memory)
	(pause)
	(try [look])

%% (narrate divining $) defined in world.dg so it’s different for each skeleton

(grammar [hyperventilate] for itself)
(prevent [hyperventilate])
	(#n-ziqqurat is unvisited)
	That seems very unlikely to help with your thesis. In fact, it would probably make things worse.
(perform [hyperventilate])
	(try [wake up])

(prevent [wake up])
	~(dreaming)
	You’ve gotten quite good at waking up on command, but right now you’re already awake.
	(if) (#n-ziqqurat is visited) (then)
		You’ll need to (span @bold) divine to get back into the memories.
	(endif)

(perform [wake up])
	(narrate awakening)
	(select player #p-real)
	(pause)
	(try [look])

(narrate awakening)
	(select)
		Returning to the real world is a bit like waking up from a lucid dream. Your method isn’t the most dignified, but it is highly reliable—all you have to do is hyperventilate. If your physical body mirrors it, even a little, it’ll shock you awake.
		(par) So you pause, close your eyes, and start to force as much air out of your lungs as you can in fast, sharp breaths. Soon your head starts to swim, and...
	(or)
		Your method worked last time, so it should work again. So you pause, close your eyes, and start to force as much air out of your lungs as you can in fast, sharp breaths. Soon your head starts to swim, and...
	(or)
		You pause, close your eyes, and start to hyperventilate, until...
	(stopping)

%% We also cut off all NPC interaction in the dream world
(prevent [talk to (animate $)]) (when dreaming)
(prevent [tell (animate $) to | $]) (when dreaming)
(prevent [give/show $ to (animate $)]) (when dreaming)
(prevent $Action)
	*(action $Action requires $Obj to be touched)
	(animate $Obj)
	(when dreaming)

(when dreaming)
	(dreaming)
	Humans in memories are tricky things. The psychic impressions only record what actually happened; everything else is happening inside your head, as your brain fills in how things (emph {ought}) to work. (par)
	But people are harder to predict than spears and doors. If you do much of anything beyond looking at them, they get hazy and indistinct. You’re hoping to find a way to fix this, eventually.

%% And a special case that doesn’t require touching the person, but still falls under this
(prevent [take $Obj])
	(dreaming)
	($Obj is #heldby $Other)
	~(current player $Other)
	(The $Other) (is $Other) frozen like a statue, and (its $Other) hand won’t release (the $Obj).

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                       Ropes and Climbing                      %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% First, we implement the rope as an item you can carry around

#rope
(name *) nylon rope
(uncountable *)
(* is #in #backpack)
(* is handled) %% Initial appearance
(descr *) Light enough to carry around; strong enough to hold your weight.
(item *)

(puzzle *)
(solved *)
	(upper rope connected) (or) (#dig is visited)

(prevent [tie * to *]) You’ve got a decent amount of practice tying ropes around things, but just making a loop is less useful.
(instead of [tie $Obj to *])
	~($Obj = *) %% Don’t recurse forever!
	(try [tie * to $Obj])

(prevent [untie * | $])
	You would need to tie the rope to something first, before you can worry about untying it.
(prevent [tie * to (item $Obj)])
	You give it a try, but the rope is quite thick—it’s meant to be knotted around something bigger than (the $Obj).
(prevent [tie * to (current player $)])
	None of the elevations around here are higher than the length of your rope, so that kind of climbing won’t be much help. Better to tie the rope to something solid, and then climb up and down it.

(perform [tie * to #post])
	(select)
		You tie a quick hitch around the survey post, then give the rope a couple of experimental tugs. Seems strong enough to hold your weight. The other end trails down into the excavation below.
	(or)
		You hitch the rope to the survey post, and let the end trail down into the excavation.
	(stopping)
	(now) (* is nowhere)
	(now) (upper rope connected)
	(update environment around player)
	(notice #rope-u)

(prevent [tie * to #cart])
	(#cart is unlocked)
	With the wheels unlocked, it won’t make a good anchor. And for dragging it around, the handle is easier without a rope.

(perform [tie * to #cart])
	(current room #deeppit)
	You check the locks on the wheels, loop the rope around underneath, double-check the locks on the wheels, and toss the end down into the pit. Then triple-check the locks on the wheels, just to be sure.
	(now) (* is nowhere)
	(now) (lower rope connected)
	(update environment around player)
	(notice #rope-d)

(perform [tie * to $Obj])
	You test a quick loop around (the $Obj), but without a convenient pit nearby, it wouldn’t do much good.
	(tick) (stop)

%% Then, we implement two separate “rope doors” you can climb

(name (rope door $)) nylon rope
(uncountable *(rope door $))
(supposed to be nowhere *(rope door $))
(door *(rope door $))
(descr (rope door $)) Strong and well-tied to support your weight. You should be able to climb up and down it without difficulty.
(item *(rope door $)) %% Though they can’t be taken

%% Since it’s the obvious verb
(instead of [climb (rope door $Obj)]) (try [enter $Obj])

(narrate leaving $Room $Dir)
	(from $Room go $Dir to $Rope)
	(rope door $Rope)
	(if) ($Dir = #down) (or) (from $Room go #down to $Dir) (then)
		(select)
			You grab hold of the rope and start to descend. It ends up being more of an undignified scramble than the graceful slide you were envisioning, but you arrive at the bottom more-or-less intact.
		(or)
			You grab hold of the rope and make your way down.
		(stopping)
	(else)
		(select)
			You grab hold of the rope with both hands, brace your feet against the side of the pit, and make your way up.
		(or)
			You grab hold of the rope and make your way up.
		(stopping)
	(endif)

%% Don’t allow doing normal rope things with it
(prevent [take (rope door $)])
	You would need to (link) {untie the rope} before taking it.
(prevent [tie (rope door $Obj) to $Obj])
	It currently hangs down in a way that’s easy to climb. Making a loop wouldn’t help there.
(prevent [tie (rope door $) to (current player $)])
	The pit isn’t deep enough for that type of climbing to help. Better just to (link) {climb the rope} as it stands now.
(prevent [tie (rope door $) to $])
	It currently hangs down in a way that’s easy to climb. Tying it to other things wouldn’t help there.

%% Commutativity
(instead of [tie $Obj to (rope door $Door)])
	~(rope door $Obj)
	(try [tie $Door to $Obj])
(instead of [untie $Obj from (rope door $Door)])
	~(rope door $Obj)
	(try [untie $Door from $Obj])

%% Upper rope, tied to the post
#rope-u
(rope door *)

(appearance * #in #abovedig) Your ({rope}) is tied to the post, offering an easy climb down to the east.
(appearance * #in #dig) Your ({rope}) trails down from the origin marker, offering an easy climb up to the west.

(prevent [untie * | $])
	~(current room #abovedig)
	The rope is anchored to the post above the dig site. You’d need to (link [go to above the dig site]) {go there} to untie it.

(prevent [untie * from $Obj])
	~($Obj = #post)
	The rope is currently tied to the post, not (the $Obj).

(perform [untie * | $])
	You undo your hitch and pull the rope back into a loose coil.
	(now) ~(upper rope connected)
	(now) (* is nowhere)
	(current player $Player)
	(now) (#rope is #heldby $Player)
	(update environment around player)
	(if) (current focus *) (then) (now) (current focus #rope) (endif)
	(notice #rope)

%% Lower rope, tied to the cart
#rope-d
(rope door *)

%% (appearance * #in #deeppit) Your ({rope}) is tied to the cart, offering a route down into the chamber below. %% Moved to cart appearance
(appearance * #in #tunnel) Your ({rope}) is anchored to the cart up above, giving you a way to climb back out.

(prevent [untie * | $])
	~(current room #deeppit)
	The rope is anchored to the cart up above. You’d need to (link [go to the deep pit]) {go there} to untie it.

(prevent [untie * from $Obj])
	~($Obj = #cart)
	The rope is currently tied to the cart, not (the $Obj).

(perform [untie * | $])
	You undo your hitch and pull the rope back into a loose coil.
	(now) ~(lower rope connected)
	(now) (* is nowhere)
	(current player $Player)
	(now) (#rope is #heldby $Player)
	(update environment around player)
	(if) (current focus *) (then) (now) (current focus #rope) (endif)
	(notice #rope)

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                        Carts and Pushing                      %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% Really this one could be handled in world.dg, it’s not *that* huge, but I’ve been putting any object with more than a page of specific rules in here

#cart
(name *) equipment cart
(* is #in #looseearth)
(dict *) wheeled wheel wheels handle crate crates
%% Making these two universal; the (before $) rules below will make sure they only apply in the right states
(pushable *) %% (* is unlocked)
(actor supporter *) %% (* is locked)
(lockable *)
~(openable *)
(descr *)
	The archaeologists must use this to move their heavy equipment between the pits. Currently it’s loaded with heavy plastic crates, big enough to stand on, and the wheels
	(if) (* is locked) (then)
		are locked to keep it from rolling around.
	(else)
		are unlocked to make it easy to push around.
	(endif)
(appearance *)
	A ({wheeled equipment cart})
	(if) (* is locked) (then)
		is securely parked
	(else)
		sits
	(endif)
	off to one side.
	(if) (lower rope connected) (then)
		A ({rope}) is tied around it, offering a route down into the chamber below.
	(endif)

(puzzle *)
(solved *) %% Rope has been untied
	~(upper rope connected) (#dig is visited)
(solved *) %% Make sure it stays solved after use
	(#tunnel is visited)

(before [climb *])
	(* is unlocked)
	(first try [lock *])
(before [push * $])
	(* is locked)
	(first try [unlock *])

%% Now we need to override stdlib a bit to make (un)locking not need a key

(prevent [lock/unlock * with $])
	This isn’t the sort of lock that needs a key—just ({push the little lever}) by the wheels.

(perform [lock *])
	You flip the lever and lock the wheels in place. Now it would take well more than your strength to move it—it should easily hold your weight.
	(now) (* is locked)
(perform [unlock *])
	You flip the lever and unlock the wheels. Now you can push it easily from place to place.
	(now) (* is unlocked)

%% Simplest option is to just do this automatically instead of making the player do it; by this point all the puzzles are solved and there’s no reason to make it difficult
(before [unlock *])
	(lower rope connected)
	(first try [untie #rope-d])

%% And don’t let it be brought into or out of the pits
(prevent [leave #deeppit #north with *]) You could probably shove the cart into the pit, but why? It would just anger the archaeologists—plus, it might damage the artifacts!
(prevent [leave #dig #west with *]) Even on wheels, the cart is far too heavy to push up a slope like that.

%% For people who take the descriptions very literally
#cartlever
(name *) little lever
(* is #partof #cart)
(descr *) (link [push the lever]) { Push this } to lock or unlock the cart’s wheels.

(instead of [lock * | $])
	\( locking the cart \) (line)
	(try [lock #cart])
(instead of [unlock * | $])
	\( unlocking the cart \) (line)
	(try [unlock #cart])
(instead of [push/pull *])
	(if) (#cart is locked) (then)
		\( unlocking the cart \) (line)
		(try [unlock #cart])
	(else)
		\( locking the cart \) (line)
		(try [lock #cart])
	(endif)

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                        Books and Topics                       %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

#dictionary
(name *) (emph {Pocket Encyclopedia of the Neo-Assyrian World})
(dict *) (just) Pocket Encyclopedia of the Neo-Assyrian World %% The closure in a (name $) causes problems on Z-machine, so we circumvent it
(dict *) book dictionary neo assyrian encyclopaedia
(heads *) encyclopedia
(consultable *)
(item *)
(* is #in #backpack)
(descr *)
	When it comes to looking up obscure names and terms, you can’t beat the reliability of a paper book.
	
	(collect $Topic)
		*(topic $Topic)
		($Topic is unlocked)
	(into $List)
	(if) (nonempty $List) (then)
		This one has entries on (topic-listing $List), among others.
		
		(select)
			(div @tutorial) {
				To look one up,
				(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
					click on the underlined words
				(else)
					try (span @command) { LOOK UP \[ topic \] IN DICTIONARY }, or just (span @command) { LOOK UP \[ topic \] }
				(endif)
				.
			}
		(or)
		(stopping)
	(else)
		(select)
			(div @tutorial) {
				Once you’ve come across some obscure names and terms, examining the encyclopedia will list them all.
			}
		(or)
		(stopping)
	(endif)
(instead of [read *]) (try [examine *])

(puzzle *)
(solved *) (* is examined)

%% List bare names, bolding ones that haven’t been read yet
(topic-listing $List)
	(listing $List {(topic-item $_)} @and [] 0)
(topic-item $Topic)
	(collect words)
		look up (name $Topic) in the dictionary
	(into $Words)
	(if) ($Topic is visited) (then)
		(link $Words) { (span @topic) (the $Topic) }
	(else)
		(link $Words) { (span @bold) { (span @topic) (the $Topic) } }
	(endif)

%% Convenience
(unlock $Topic) (now) ($Topic is unlocked)

(grammar [look up [topic]] for [consult about $])
~(action [consult about $Obj] requires $Obj to be present)
(perform [consult about $Topic])
	(if) (player can see #dictionary) (then)
		\( in the pocket encyclopedia \) (line)
		(try [consult #dictionary about $Topic])
	(else)
		You don’t have any reference works on hand.
	(endif)

(style class @dictionary)
	font-style: italic;
	margin-top: 1em;
	margin-bottom: 1em;

(perform [consult #dictionary about $Topic])
	(topic $Topic)
	(div @dictionary) {
		(span @bold) { (name $Topic) : }
		(dictionary entry for $Topic)
	}
	(now) ($Topic is visited)
(perform [consult #dictionary about $])
	You flip through the encyclopedia, but don’t see an entry for that.
	(div @tutorial) { (link) { Read the dictionary } to see what entries it contains. }

%% Now for the actual contents!

(style class @topic) %% TODO: Z-machine
	text-decoration: underline;
	text-decoration-style: dotted;

(supposed to be nowhere *(topic $))

#t-babylon
(name *) Babylon
(dict *) babylonia
(proper *)
(topic *)
(dictionary entry for *)
	At one time, the capital of the Babylonian Empire; in the time of (span @topic) Nineveh , though, it was a vassal state of the (span @topic) {Neo-Assyrians}. Even so, it remained a famous center of art, culture, and religion, and never fully accepted Assyrian dominance. In the modern day it’s known as Hillah, Iraq.
	(unlock #t-nineveh)
	(unlock #t-empire)

#t-nineveh
(name *) Nineveh
(* is unlocked) %% UNLOCKED BY DEFAULT
(proper *)
(topic *)
(dictionary entry for *)
	The main capital of the (span @topic) {Neo-Assyrian Empire}. For all its military strength, it never quite managed to supplant (span @topic) Babylon as the artistic and cultural heart of Mesopotamia. In the modern day it’s known as Mosul, Iraq.
	(unlock #t-empire)
	(unlock #t-babylon)

#t-empire
(name *) Neo-Assyrian Empire
(dict *) neo assyrian neo-assyrians
(heads *) empire
(* is unlocked) %% UNLOCKED BY DEFAULT
(topic *)
(dictionary entry for *)
	An Iron Age state that was briefly the largest and most powerful empire the world had ever seen, before it came to a sudden and violent end around 600 BCE. Many later empires were inspired by the Neo-Assyrians’ brutality and their goal of complete world domination.

#t-siin-ahhe-eriba
(name *) Siin-ahhe-eriba
(dict *) siin ahhe eriba sennacherib
(heads *) eriba
(proper *)
(topic *)
(dictionary entry for *)
	King of the (span @topic) {Neo-Assyrian Empire} from 705 to 681 BCE, best known for renovating the capital at Nineveh, and for destroying the city of (span @topic) Babylon after rebels there killed his eldest son. His name means “(no space) (span @topic) Siin has replaced the brothers”, and is transcribed in Hebrew as “Sennacherib”.
	(unlock #t-empire)
	(unlock #t-babylon)
	(unlock #t-nineveh)
	(unlock #t-gods)

#t-urad-mullissi
(name *) Urad-Mullissi
(dict *) urad mullissi adrammelech
(heads *) urad
(proper *)
(topic *)
(dictionary entry for *)
	A son of (span @topic) Siin-ahhe-eriba . He was the obvious choice for crown prince, as the eldest surviving son of the king’s primary wife, and he held the title for some time—until the king suddenly changed his mind and declared Asshur-ahu-iddina his heir instead. His name means “servant of (span @topic) Mullissu (no space)”, and is transcribed \( somewhat mangled \) in Hebrew as “Adrammelech”.
	(unlock #t-siin-ahhe-eriba)
	(unlock #t-asshur-ahu-iddina)
	(unlock #t-gods)

#t-asshur-ahu-iddina
(name *) Asshur-ahu-iddina
(dict *) asshur ahu iddina esarhaddon
(heads *) iddina
(proper *)
(topic *)
(dictionary entry for *)
	King of the (span @topic) {Neo-Assyrian Empire} from 681 to 669 BCE, best known for conquering Egypt and for the paranoia that drove him to assassinate several of his own governors. He was the youngest son of (span @topic) Siin-ahhe-eriba by a secondary wife, very far down the line of succession, and it seemed unlikely he would ever inherit the throne—but his mother’s machinations led the king to choose him as heir over his older brothers. His name means “(no space) (span @topic) Asshur has given a brother”, and is transcribed in Hebrew as “Esarhaddon”.
	(unlock #t-siin-ahhe-eriba)
	(unlock #t-gods)
	(unlock #t-empire)

#t-gods
(name *) gods and goddesses
(dict *) god goddess siin mullissu mullissi asshur nabuu anshar marduk
(heads *) siin mullissu mullissi asshur %% Since these also appear in names
(plural *)
(topic *)
(dictionary entry for *)
	The many gods of the (span @topic) {Neo-Assyrian Empire} included:
	(div @list) {
		(div @listitem) { (bullet) Anshar, titan of the horizon and ancestor of the gods }
		(div @listitem) { (bullet) Asshur, god of Assyria; (span @topic) {Siin-ahhe-eriba} claimed he was the same as Anshar }
		(div @listitem) { (bullet) Marduk, god of kingship and (span @topic) {Babylonia} }
		(div @listitem) { (bullet) Mullissu, goddess of love and destiny, wife of Asshur }
		(div @listitem) { (bullet) Nabuu, god of writing and knowledge, vizier of Marduk }
		(div @listitem) { (bullet) Siin, god of the moon, fertility, and dairy products }
	}
	Most Assyrian and Babylonian names were full sentences, and including a god in your child’s name was a good way to ensure that god would favor them.
	(unlock #t-babylon)
	(unlock #t-empire)
	(unlock #t-siin-ahhe-eriba)

#t-ziggurat
(name *) ziggurats
(dict *) ziggurat
(plural *)
(topic *)
(dictionary entry for *)
	Ziggurats were pyramid-shaped towers built out of a stack of terraces, each smaller than the one beneath it. The most famous one was the Etemenanki \( “Temple of the Foundation of Heaven and Earth” \) in Babylon, which was probably the inspiration for the Tower of Babel; ancient sources claimed it was almost 100 meters tall, though modern engineers think two-thirds of that is more likely.
	(if) (interpreter can embed @ziggurat) (then)
		(par) (embed resource @ziggurat)
		(par) \( Model from the Pergamon Museum in Berlin. \)
	(endif)

(define resource @ziggurat) etemenanki.jpg; A modern model of the Etemenanki.

%% And at the end:
(*(topic $) is locked) %% Lock all topics unless otherwise specified

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                           Vignettes                           %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

(room *(vignette $))

(narrate leaving $Room $Dir)
	(from $Room go $Dir to $Next)
	(vignette $Next)
	You move toward the traces of motion, and the dream shifts... (par)

(style class @vignette)
	font-style: italic;
	margin-left: 2em;
	margin-right: 2em;
	margin-top: 1em;
	margin-bottom: 1em;

(narrate entering (vignette $Room))
	(div @vignette) {
		(now) (currently italic)
		(narrate vignette for $Room)
		(now) ~(currently italic)
	}
	(if) ($Room is unexamined) (then) %% We repurpose this flag for whether the vignette has been shown, because the ($ is visited) flag gets set too early
		(pause)
		(select)
			You jerk yourself free of the memory, and everything freezes again.
		(or)
			You pull yourself out of the memory with a (emph {snap}), and everything goes still.
		(or)
			You snap yourself out of the memory and take a look around.
		(then at random)
		(select) %% Little tutorial
			Now you have the freedom to look around the tableau; you can’t change the past, but you can search and poke and prod to your heart’s content. A gap to the south leads back to the memory palace.
		(or)
		(stopping)
	(else)
		You (select) jerk (or) pull (or) snap (at random) back out of the memory.
	(endif)
	(par) (try [look])

(narrate failing to leave (vignette $Room) $)
	Each memory only captures a brief moment in time: what the person saw, heard, and felt in those scant few seconds. Everything beyond that is lost in a dark haze.
	(if) *(from $Room go $Alt to $Next) (room $Next) (then) %% Should always be true
		(par) If you want to go back to the memory palace, there’s a tear in the haze to the ($Alt), like a hole crudely ripped in a cloth backdrop.
	(else)
		ERROR: There’s no way to leave. Report this please!
	(endif)

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                              Meta                             %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

(grammar [about/credit/credits] for [about])
(command [about])

(perform [about])
	(div @meta) (story credits)
